  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 346 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge extends from Arizona, New Mexico, and Texas \nnorthward to Kansas and Nebraska. Winds across north central Kansas \nand south central and central Nebraska are mostly light and \nvariable. Winds are lighter than expected but may increase some out \nof the south later this afternoon. Low-level moisture is hanging \naround the area longer than expected today (with very little wind to \nmix in drier air) with dewpoints mostly in the 70s and 80s. High \ntemperatures today are expected to reach the mid 90s to low triple \ndigits. These conditions have resulted in heat index values across \nthe area generally ranging from around 105 to near 120 degrees! A \nHeat Advisory is in effect for the whole area and continues into \nMonday evening. Low temperatures tonight are expected to be in the \n70s with dewpoint temperatures in the 60s and 70s. There is some \nconcern that dewpoint temperatures will be higher than what is \ncurrently in the forecast, especially if drier air doesn't move into \nthe area. This may lead to fog development as temperatures cool. \nWinds will be fairly light (mainly between 5 to 10 MPH but may be \ncloser to 5 MPH) out of the south. These overnight conditions will \nresult in limited relief from the heat. If moisture is higher than \nexpected tonight, overnight lows may also be higher than what is in \nthe forecast.\n\nThe upper ridge will be more centered over the region on Sunday. \nWinds will be a little stronger than the previous day Sunday \nafternoon (southerly around 10 to 20 MPH with gusts up to around 25 \nMPH). Sunny to partly cloudy skies will be present. Temperatures \nSunday afternoon are expected to heat up to the mid/upper 90s to \naround 105 degrees. The amount of moisture that will be present for \nSunday is still somewhat uncertain with the models not in agreement. \nBased on the moisture hanging around longer than expected today, \ntend to think that moisture values will be on the higher end. \nTemperatures on Sunday are expected to be slightly hotter than \ntoday. An Extreme Heat Warning may be needed for Sunday. Low \ntemperatures for Sunday night will likely be slightly warmer than \nthe previous night with some isolated areas in north central Kansas \nperhaps with lows around 80 degrees. There is a very slight (15% to \nnear 20% chance) of showers and storms Sunday evening across \nnorthern portions of the area (mainly north of I-80). There is some \nmodel disagreement on where these storms will occur but the main \nrisk with these storms will be wind gusts up to around 60 MPH. \n\nMonday has the potential to be the hottest day but there is also a \nlot of uncertainty with regards to temperature and moisture. \nGenerally expecting high temperatures to range from 97 to 103 \ndegrees. Some of the hotter models have temperatures ranging from \naround 105 to 110+ degrees! Tend to think these hotter temperatures \nare not likely with a weak cold front with easterly winds expected \nto begin moving into the area on Monday. The Heat Advisory ends at \n10 PM Monday evening. Beginning Monday evening and continuing into \nFriday, there are various chances of showers and storms across \nportions of the area as shortwaves troughs move overhead. High \ntemperatures will be cooler beginning Tuesday with the arrival of a \ncold front.",
